WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.163
Integer values often control indexes, loop
bounds, counts, offsets,

2
00:00:04.243 --> 00:00:06.229
and remaining-length calculations.

3
00:00:06.309 --> 00:00:10.283
That makes integer safety part of every
parser, serializer, buffer walker,

4
00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:13.467
table lookup, and loop that processes
variable-size data.

5
00:00:13.547 --> 00:00:15.533
The numeric value is not just a number;

6
00:00:15.613 --> 00:00:18.159
it is a permission to move through memory
or data.

7
00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:20.970
Off-by-one mistakes can invalidate bounds
checks.

8
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.471
A loop that allows an index equal to the
length,

9
00:00:23.551 --> 00:00:25.538
a copy that forgets terminator space,

10
00:00:25.618 --> 00:00:29.840
or a range check that subtracts before
verifying order can all cross the intended

11
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:30.478
boundary.

12
00:00:30.558 --> 00:00:34.718
Review should look at both the check and
the arithmetic used to reach the checked

13
00:00:34.798 --> 00:00:35.170
value.

14
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.734
Unsigned counters deserve special
attention.

15
00:00:37.814 --> 00:00:41.973
Decrementing an unsigned value can wrap if
the termination condition is wrong.

16
00:00:42.053 --> 00:00:46.212
A remaining-length calculation can become
large if code subtracts more than is

17
00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:46.913
available.

18
00:00:46.993 --> 00:00:51.029
The fact that a value is nonnegative by
type does not mean it represents a valid

19
00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:52.102
remaining amount.

20
00:00:52.182 --> 00:00:54.913
Negative values, maximum values, sentinel
values,

21
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:59.277
and parsed numeric fields require explicit
handling before they become indexes,

22
00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:00.413
counts, or offsets.

23
00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:04.466
A parser should validate length fields
before using them to skip, allocate,

24
00:01:04.546 --> 00:01:06.284
copy, index, or advance a cursor.

25
00:01:06.364 --> 00:01:10.027
The validated range should remain
connected to the data it describes.

26
00:01:10.107 --> 00:01:13.894
Loop invariants should make it clear that
indexes remain within bounds.

27
00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:17.140
A reviewer should be able to see why the
index starts in range,

28
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.441
why each update keeps it in range, and why
the loop stops before an invalid access.

29
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:25.991
If that reasoning depends on several
scattered conversions or unchecked additions,

30
00:01:26.071 --> 00:01:27.499
the loop is harder to trust.

31
00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:29.814
Operationally, test the edge of the range.

32
00:01:29.894 --> 00:01:33.805
Empty input, one element, maximum accepted
length, one above the maximum,

33
00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:38.231
malformed numeric fields, and early
termination all exercise the arithmetic that

34
00:01:38.311 --> 00:01:39.801
ordinary examples may skip.

35
00:01:39.881 --> 00:01:44.289
Parser safety improves when boundary
behavior is part of normal testing rather than

36
00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:45.300
an afterthought.
